alex: Heads-up — iOS client (URLSession SSE) is intermittently stalling when responses contain partial JSON chunks. Also seeing token refresh + reconnect race that results in duplicate connections. Repro steps and logs below.

alex: repro: open stream, wait ~5s, send long response that gets split across TCP frames, then trigger token expiry. Sometimes client throws EOF during JSON decode and then immediately reconnects while previous connection still alive.

alex: logs:
```
2026-03-17T12:00:01Z Stream chunk received: "{\"id\":1, \"text\":\"start...\"}"
2026-03-17T12:00:02Z JSONDecodeError: Unexpected EOF
2026-03-17T12:00:02Z Token refresh triggered, reconnecting
2026-03-17T12:00:03Z New connection established (duplicate)
```

jordan: Are you decoding each incoming chunk with JSONDecoder directly? That will fail on fragments. You need to reassemble complete messages or use a streaming-capable parser.

maria: Swift's JSONDecoder expects complete top-level objects. If the server can be configured, NDJSON (newline-delimited JSON) is easiest to handle on the client. Otherwise implement a small reassembly layer that emits complete objects. :thinking_face: 

alex: Can't change server immediately — customers rely on hosted API. Need a robust client-side workaround.

jordan: Client-side reassembly idea (very small): buffer bytes, look for a framing delimiter (\n). If the server doesn't send newlines, you can try detecting top-level object boundaries but that's fragile for nested braces. Example reassembly for NDJSON: 
```swift
var buf = Data()
func onChunk(_ d: Data) {
  buf.append(d)
  while let idx = buf.firstIndex(of: UInt8(10)) { // \n
    let line = buf[..<idx]
    buf.removeSubrange(...idx)
    processLine(Data(line))
  }
}
func processLine(_ data: Data) {
  do {
    let obj = try JSONDecoder().decode(Event.self, from: data)
    handle(obj)
  } catch {
    // log and drop or buffer more depending on policy
  }
}
```

maria: If you must handle arbitrary split JSON without newline framing, consider using a streaming JSON parser (e.g., SwiftNIO + a JSON parser that supports incremental feeds) rather than trying to match braces. Also watch multibyte UTF-8 boundaries — splitting in the middle of an emoji will corrupt decoding unless you buffer by bytes.

maria: Backpressure: if you're using Combine to bridge URLSession dataTask events, make sure you don't shuttle each tiny chunk to the decoder on the main thread. Use buffer + receive(on:) to avoid overwhelming CPU and to allow upstream TCP buffer to apply backpressure. Example: 
```swift
publisher
  .map { Data($0) }
  .buffer(size: 16, prefetch: .byRequest, whenFull: .dropNewest)
  .receive(on: DispatchQueue.global(qos: .userInitiated))
  .sink { data in /* reassembly/decode */ }
```

jordan: Auth race: ensure a single reconnect gate. Token refresh should be serialized so only one in-flight refresh triggers a reconnect. Use an atomic flag or a serial DispatchQueue and cancel or wait for previous connection teardown before creating a new one. Pseudocode: 
```swift
enum ConnState { case idle, connecting, connected, tearingDown }
var state = ConnState.idle // access on serial queue
func refreshAndReconnect() { serialQueue.async {
  guard state != .connecting && state != .tearingDown else { return }
  state = .tearingDown
  currentTask?.cancel()
  // wait for canceled completion -> then start new connect
  startConnect()
}}
```

kai: Also add exponential backoff jitter to reconnect attempts to avoid thundering herd if lots of clients refresh at once. Keep a short but bounded retry window for interactive streams.

andy: Tests: add unit tests that feed the reassembler: (1) JSON split across multiple chunks, (2) split multibyte UTF-8 sequences (emoji), (3) multiple objects in a single chunk, (4) rapid reconnect while decoding.

alex: Great suggestions. Plan: implement NDJSON reassembly fallback, Combine buffer + background decode, and a reconnect gate with cancel-on-refresh. I'll push a draft PR and link tests.

